+            /* Dado project patch (collections/vendor/enet/enet-timeout-shift-ub.patch):
+             * the retry test below was `(1u << (outgoingCommand->sendAttempts - 1))`,
+             * whose shift count is unbounded. `sendAttempts` is an `enet_uint16`
+             * that this file increments once per retransmit and never caps, so at
+             * 33 the count reaches 32 and shifting a 32-bit `unsigned int` by its
+             * own width is undefined (C99 6.5.7p3); at 0 the count is -1, which is
+             * undefined for the same reason. UBSan reports the first as `shift
+             * exponent 32 is too large`, and the practical damage is worse than the
+             * report: with the count masked to five bits the comparison goes false
+             * again for 33..37 attempts and true at 38, so the `timeoutMinimum`
+             * disconnect flaps instead of latching and a dead peer lingers toward
+             * `timeoutMaximum` — six times longer than configured, and differently
+             * per architecture and optimisation level.
+             *
+             * Reachable in this fork specifically. Upstream ENet doubles
+             * `roundTripTimeout` on every timeout, so 33 retries would take 2^33 ms;
+             * this fork replaced that with the constant `roundTripTime + 4 *
+             * roundTripTimeVariance` a few lines below ("Replaced exponential
+             * backoff time with something more linear"), so on a low-RTT link the
+             * count passes 32 in well under a second — long before the five-second
+             * `timeoutMinimum` gate can open. Any peer that stops acknowledging
+             * under a live reliable send reaches it: a crash, a kill, a pulled
+             * cable. `example/net` met it on loopback.
+             *
+             * Clamping the exponent is the whole edit. For 1..32 attempts the value
+             * is bit-identical to what the line always computed; past that it
+             * saturates at `1u << 31`, which keeps the test monotone in the retry
+             * count — the property the undefined version lost — and 0 attempts now
+             * answers false rather than shifting by -1. The linear backoff itself
+             * is left alone: it is this fork's deliberate choice and changing it is
+             * a behavioural decision, not a defect fix. */
+            enet_uint32 timeoutExponent = outgoingCommand->sendAttempts;
+            if (timeoutExponent > 32) {
+                timeoutExponent = 32;
+            }
+
             if (peer->earliestTimeout != 0 &&
                 (ENET_TIME_DIFFERENCE(host->serviceTime, peer->earliestTimeout) >= peer->timeoutMaximum ||
-                ((1u << (outgoingCommand->sendAttempts - 1)) >= peer->timeoutLimit &&
+                (timeoutExponent >= 1 && (1u << (timeoutExponent - 1)) >= peer->timeoutLimit &&
                 ENET_TIME_DIFFERENCE(host->serviceTime, peer->earliestTimeout) >= peer->timeoutMinimum))
